From dc645a3dab207c9560b2d3329be433f9f5362ec3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eden PS4 <eden-ps4@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 05:35:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PS4: fastmem, through direct memory aliasing

The PS4 maps the same direct memory twice for an app once sceKernelEnableDmemAliasing has been
called (03.83's probe on a console). The emulated DRAM gets a fastmem arena (the largest of 2^39
down to 2^32 the kernel reserves), its pages mapped lazily: a fault there is resolved from the
application's page table when the 16 KiB page's four guest pages are ordinary memory back to back
in the DRAM (the DRAM chunk's direct memory mapped there, up to 512 KiB at a time), and declined
otherwise (dynarmic's fallback, the code recompiled without fastmem there).

- common/orbis_memory: the arena, its lock and invalidation, and ResolveFault, one resolver for
  the sparse vectors' pages, the on-demand DRAM's chunks and the arena's pages.
- common/host_memory: the arena reserved (EDEN_PS4_FASTMEM=0 goes without); Map, Unmap and
  Protect taking rights away invalidate; released before the DRAM. IsFastmemEnabled: an arena.
- core/memory: page table changes made with the arena locked, so no fault maps a page from
  entries about to change.
- dynarmic: the POSIX exception handler on the PS4, with its FreeBSD signal context, asking the
  port's resolver first, treating only arena faults as fastmem accesses, logging nothing; A64's
  fastmem_address_space_bits held to the arena's.
